  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1215 AM CDT Sat Sep 26 2026\n\nMid-level ridging will remain the dominant feature across South \nTexas through the weekend and into early next week, maintaining \ngenerally hot and mostly dry conditions. While an isolated sea \nbreeze shower or thunderstorm cannot be ruled out, most locations \nshould remain dry through Monday.\n\nAttention then turns to the evolving mid-level pattern beginning \nTuesday and especially Wednesday through the latter half of the \nweek. Global guidance continues to disagree on the evolution and \nplacement of a mid-level low as it interacts with a mid-level ridge \nanchored across the southeastern U.S. Despite these differences, the \noverall trend suggests the low will ride northward around the \nwestern and northern periphery of the ridge. This would keep the \nstronger forcing and much of the associated PVA north of South Texas.\n\nEven with the better mid-level forcing displaced to the north, \nincreasing moisture and sufficient instability should support a \ngradual increase in convective coverage beginning Tuesday. Low to \nmoderate rain chances Tuesday become more widespread Wednesday as \nmoisture deepens across the region/ Moderate chances for showers and \nthunderstorms are the expected Thursday through Saturday.\n\nConsiderable uncertainty remains regarding the exact evolution and \nthe mid-level low and ridge interaction, and relatively small \nchanges in the track of the low could affect the amount of forcing \nthat reaches South Texas. Therefore, confidence remains low that the \ncold front will make it this far south. Subsequent model runs should \nhelp refine the details.\n\nTemperatures will remain above normal through early next week. \nIncreasing cloud cover and convective coverage during the latter \nhalf of the week may provide some moderation to daytime \ntemperatures, though warm and humid conditions will persist. Look \nfor afternoon highs in the mid to upper 90s drop to mid-80s to 90s \nnext weekend. Overnight lows in the low to upper 70s is expected to \ndrop a few degrees as well. A moderate to major heat risk continues \nthrough this weekend into next Wednesday so please continue to \npractice heat safety.\n\nThe potential for minor coastal flooding will begin to increase \nSunday evening and may persist through at least the middle of next \nweek. P-ETSS guidance indicates water levels approaching the dunes \naround times of high tide beginning Sunday evening, followed by a \ngradual upward trend through at least Wednesday. Astronomical tides \nwill also be elevated following the full moon Saturday evening. \nThere remain some limiting factors that lower confidence in the \nextent of any coastal flooding. Swell periods are currently expected \nto remain relatively short at less the 7 seconds, while variable \nwinds may remain too weak to provide a substantial additional \nonshore push. Nevertheless, given the combination of elevated \nastronomical tides and the upward trend in the forecast water \nlevels, minor coastal flooding will be possible around the times of \nhigh tide beginning Sunday evening. Trends will continue to be \nmonitored over the next several forecast cycles.",
